## Distributional Partial Derivatives of the Characteristic Function of the Open First Quadrant

Let $f = \chi_Q$ where $Q = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^2 : x_1 > 0,\; x_2 > 0\}$. We compute the distributional partial derivatives $\partial_1 f$ and $\partial_2 f$.

### Setup

For a test function $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^2)$, the distributional derivative is defined by

$$\langle \partial_i f, \varphi \rangle := -\langle f, \partial_i \varphi \rangle = -\int_Q \partial_i \varphi(x)\, dx.$$

### Computing $\partial_1 f$

$$\langle \partial_1 f, \varphi \rangle = -\int_0^\infty\!\!\int_0^\infty \partial_1 \varphi(x_1, x_2)\, dx_1\, dx_2.$$

Since $\varphi$ is compactly supported, $\varphi(x_1, x_2) \to 0$ as $x_1 \to +\infty$. Applying the fundamental theorem of calculus in the $x_1$-variable,

$$-\int_0^\infty \partial_1 \varphi(x_1, x_2)\, dx_1 = -\Big[\varphi(x_1, x_2)\Big]_{x_1=0}^{x_1 \to \infty} = \varphi(0, x_2).$$

Therefore

$$\langle \partial_1 f, \varphi \rangle = \int_0^\infty \varphi(0, x_2)\, dx_2.$$

This is the distribution given by integration against the measure concentrated on the half-line $\{0\}\times(0,\infty)$ (the positive $x_2$-axis) with unit linear density. Denoting by $H$ the Heaviside function and by $\delta_0$ the Dirac mass at $0$,

$$\boxed{\partial_1 f = \delta_0(x_1)\otimes H(x_2),}$$

i.e., $\partial_1 f$ is the measure supported on $\{x_1 = 0,\; x_2 > 0\}$.

### Computing $\partial_2 f$

By the symmetric argument (integrating in $x_2$ first),

$$\langle \partial_2 f, \varphi \rangle = -\int_0^\infty\!\!\int_0^\infty \partial_2 \varphi(x_1, x_2)\, dx_2\, dx_1 = \int_0^\infty \varphi(x_1, 0)\, dx_1.$$

Hence

$$\boxed{\partial_2 f = H(x_1)\otimes \delta_0(x_2),}$$

the measure supported on the half-line $(0,\infty)\times\{0\}$ (the positive $x_1$-axis) with unit linear density.

### Mixed second derivative (for completeness)

$$\langle \partial_1\partial_2 f, \varphi\rangle = \langle \partial_2 f, \partial_1\varphi\rangle = \int_0^\infty \partial_1\varphi(x_1, 0)\, dx_1 = -\varphi(0,0),$$

so

$$\partial_1\partial_2 f = \delta_{(0,0)},$$

the Dirac mass at the origin. This is consistent with $\partial_1(\delta_0(x_2)\otimes H(x_1)) = \delta_0(x_1)\otimes\delta_0(x_2)$.

### Summary

Writing $f = H(x_1)\,H(x_2)$ as a product of one-dimensional Heaviside functions:

| Derivative | Result | Support |
|---|---|---|
| $\partial_1 f$ | $\delta_0(x_1)\otimes H(x_2)$ | positive $x_2$-axis $\{0\}\times(0,\infty)$ |
| $\partial_2 f$ | $H(x_1)\otimes \delta_0(x_2)$ | positive $x_1$-axis $(0,\infty)\times\{0\}$ |
| $\partial_1\partial_2 f$ | $\delta_{(0,0)}$ | origin $\{(0,0)\}$ |

Each first partial derivative is a Radon measure (a "line measure" of unit density along the corresponding boundary half-axis of $Q$), and the mixed second derivative is the Dirac point mass at the corner of the quadrant.

